  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Above average temperatures through Thursday, with a \nwarm front bringing the chance for scattered light showers tonight \ninto Thursday, before a cold front generates a round of beneficial \nrain showers Thursday night into the day on Friday.\n\nGOES satellite imagery shows a mix of high clouds and sunny skies \nacross the forecast area this afternoon. An airmass change is on the \nway with an abundance of cloud cover upstream across the Great Lakes \nregion. \n\nAn upper level ridge and surface high pressure will move east of the \nforecast area tonight. Multiple shortwave troughs will sag southward \nfrom the Mid-West to the New England, while a plume of anomalously \ndeep moisture begins to enter the region overnight. A 40kt low level \njet is expected to nose into the forecast area tonight. Forcing at \nthe nose of the jet and increasing moisture will support scattered \nshowers across western NY tonight, then spread into north-central NY \nlate tonight through Thursday morning. Overall, amounts will be \nwidely scattered and less than 0.15\".\n\nThe main axis of moisture will lift north of the region Thursday. \nDrier air and increasing southwest winds will support breezy \nconditions northeast of the Lakes, especially northeast of Lake \nErie. A tightening pressure gradient and funneling up the Lake will \nsupport wind gusts up to 30 mph across the Niagara Frontier. \nOverall, mostly dry weather is expected Thursday afternoon. \n\nA strong upper level trough will move into the Upper Great Lakes \nregion Thursday night. A cold front will move across the forecast \narea Thursday evening through Friday morning. A narrow corridor of \ndeep moisture, increasing 500mb vorticity, and a strong surface \nbaroclinic zone will support a swath of light to moderate showers \nacross the forecast area. Showers will move east of the region, with \nadditional development of scattered showers across interior western \nNY to north-central NY Friday afternoon. Forecast rainfall amounts \nwill range from 0.25-0.90\", with the highest amounts across far \nwestern NY and on the Tug Hill Plateau.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Below normal temperatures are expected early next \nweek.\n\nA 500mb trough will move across eastern Canada and the eastern Great \nLakes region early next week. A cold front is expected to usher in \ncool weather with 850mb temperatures falling to sub-zero readings. \nForecast high temperatures are in the 50s Monday and Tuesday \nwith low temperatures falling to the 30s. The airmass is dry \nwith low chances of showers.",
